ISSUE:
`@JsonInclude(NON_EMPTY, content=CUSTOM)` does not omit a Map property after all entries are filtered

### Search before asking

- [x] I searched in the [issues](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/issues) and found nothing similar.

### Describe the bug

When `@JsonInclude(value=NON_EMPTY, content=CUSTOM, contentFilter=...)` is applied to a `Map` property, a Map whose entries are all filtered out should be omitted. Instead, the property is written as an empty object: `{"stuff":{}}`.

`MapSerializer.isEmpty()` applies the content filter's `equals()` to the whole `Map`, rather than to each entry value. The property is therefore considered non-empty and written; the write path then correctly filters every entry, leaving `{}`.

### Version Information

Reproduced on current `3.x` (`3.3.0-SNAPSHOT`). The same two comparisons are also present on current `2.x`.

### Reproduction

```java
static class FooFilter {
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object other) {
        return "foo".equals(other);
    }
}

static class Bean {
    @JsonInclude(value = JsonInclude.Include.NON_EMPTY,
            content = JsonInclude.Include.CUSTOM,
            contentFilter = FooFilter.class)
    public Map<String, String> stuff = new LinkedHashMap<>();
}

Bean bean = new Bean();
bean.stuff.put("a", "foo");
bean.stuff.put("b", "foo");
String json = new ObjectMapper().writeValueAsString(bean);
// actual: {"stuff":{}}
```

A Map with at least one surviving entry is already handled correctly; for example, `{"stuff":{"b":"keep"}}`.

### Expected behavior

Once the content filter excludes every entry, the property should be treated as empty because its effective Map content is empty.

```json
{}
```

### Additional context

The write path (`serializeOptionalFields`) already compares the filter with each entry value. Only the two `isEmpty()` loops compare it with the whole `Map`.

A focused fix would pass entry values, rather than the `Map` object, to the content filter during the emptiness check. A stateful filter could therefore observe a different call count or order. Is that compatibility impact acceptable for `contentFilter`?

If the intended behavior is to omit the property once all content is filtered, I can provide a focused two-line patch with static and dynamic regression tests.

Fix the SOURCE code in this repository so the issue is resolved and the project's tests pass. Edit source files only; do NOT modify any test files. Before declaring the fix verified, confirm your test run actually exercises the code you just edited in THIS checkout -- not a separately installed copy (e.g. a non-editable `pip install` into a fresh venv resolves the published package, not your edit; same risk for a cached node_modules or local Maven/Go module install that shadows this repo). If you set up an isolated environment to run tests, install this repo in editable/local mode. Make the smallest change that works, then stop.

